          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For general air quality and allergies, 4 to 5 air changes per hour is a sensible target and is what the AHAM two-thirds sizing rule delivers. For smoke, wildfire season or asthma, aim higher, around 5 to 6 or more. Multiply the purifier's CADR by 60 and divide by your room's volume in cubic feet to see what yours delivers."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do air purifiers work faster in small rooms?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, because the same CADR divided into a smaller volume gives more air changes per hour. A 150 CFM unit cleans a 10 by 10 foot room about three times as fast as a 15 by 20 foot room. That is why a modest unit in a closed bedroom often feels more effective than a large one in an open-plan living area."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is it better to run one big purifier or two small ones?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "In a single closed room, one unit with the same total CADR does about as well as two. In a larger or oddly shaped space, or two connected rooms, two smaller units placed apart usually clean more evenly because air does not mix perfectly. For separate bedrooms, one appropriately sized unit per room works best."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long should an air purifier run before I notice a difference?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "With a correctly sized unit on high and the door closed, most people notice cleaner air within 30 to 60 minutes, especially with smoke or cooking particles. Allergy relief is more gradual and usually shows over several days to a couple of weeks of continuous running, because exposure has to stay low, not just dip once."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does ceiling height affect how long it takes?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, directly. Sizing charts and the AHAM rule assume an 8 foot ceiling. A 10 foot ceiling has 25 percent more air, so every cleaning time is about 25 percent longer at the same CADR. For tall or vaulted ceilings, calculate the actual volume and size the purifier from that, not from floor area alone."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will an air purifier clean the whole house if I leave doors open?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only very slowly, and only if it is large. A portable purifier is rated for one room, and in a whole house its air changes per hour fall to a fraction of what it gives a closed bedroom. For whole-house cleaning, a better HVAC filter with the system fan running does more, with portable units in the most-used rooms."
          }
        },
