    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A correctly sized air purifier running on high typically clears most of the airborne particles from a closed room in about 30 to 60 minutes; an undersized unit, a low fan setting or an open door can stretch that to two hours or more. You can work it out yourself: divide the room's volume in cubic feet by the purifier's CADR in cubic feet per minute to get the minutes per air change, and allow about three air changes to remove roughly 95 percent of what is floating in a well-mixed room. The numbers below show how to run that math, why the box's room-size claim can mislead you, and what keeps a room from ever staying fully clean.
        </p>

        <div id="math" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Do You Work Out the Time for Your Room?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the room's volume. Multiply length by width by ceiling height. A 12 by 15 foot bedroom with an 8 foot ceiling is 1,440 cubic feet. Then find the purifier's clean air delivery rate, or CADR, which is printed on the box or in the listing under the AHAM Verified mark. Use the smoke CADR, which is usually the lowest of the three figures and the most relevant to fine particles.
          </p>

          <p>
            Divide volume by CADR and you get the minutes it takes the purifier to deliver one room's worth of clean air. A 200 CFM unit in that 1,440 cubic foot bedroom delivers one air change roughly every 7 minutes. A 100 CFM unit takes about 14 minutes. A small desktop unit at 50 CFM takes nearly half an hour.
          </p>

          <p>
            To turn that into air changes per hour, multiply CADR by 60 and divide by the volume. The 200 CFM unit gives about 8 air changes per hour, the 100 CFM unit about 4, and the 50 CFM unit about 2. Most indoor air guidance treats 4 to 5 air changes per hour as a good target for allergies and general air quality, and more for smoke.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Room (8 ft ceiling)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Volume</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">CADR 100</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">CADR 200</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">CADR 300</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">10 x 12 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">960 cu ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 10 min</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 5 min</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 3 min</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">12 x 15 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,440 cu ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 14 min</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 7 min</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 5 min</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">15 x 20 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2,400 cu ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 24 min</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 12 min</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 8 min</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">20 x 25 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4,000 cu ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 40 min</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 20 min</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 13 min</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Those are minutes per single air change at the purifier's highest speed. The next section explains why one air change is not enough, and how many it takes before you can call the room clean.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="air-changes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Does One Air Change Actually Remove?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Less than you would think. One air change does not mean every particle has been through the filter, because clean air from the outlet mixes with dirty air in the room, and some of the air the purifier pulls in has already been cleaned. In a well-mixed room with no new pollution, each air change removes about 63 percent of what is left. That is how dilution works, and it applies to any filter, however good.
          </p>

          <p>
            The reductions stack. After two air changes roughly 86 percent of the original particles are gone, after three about 95 percent, and after five more than 99 percent. So the practical rule is to multiply the minutes per air change by three for a room that feels clean, and by five for close to the full effect.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Air Changes</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Particles Removed</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Time at 5 ACH</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Time at 2 ACH</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 63%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">12 min</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30 min</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">2</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 86%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 min</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">60 min</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">3</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 95%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">36 min</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">90 min</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">5</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 99%</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">60 min</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">150 min</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            This is the idealized case. Real rooms do not mix perfectly; corners, the space behind furniture and the air near the ceiling lag behind. New particles keep arriving from people, pets, cooking and the air leaking in from outside. In practice, a room reaches a lower steady level and stays there while the purifier runs, rather than reaching zero.
          </p>

          <p>
            It also explains why bigger is better even when the box says a small unit covers your room. Doubling the CADR halves every number in that table, which is the difference between a bedroom that is clean by the time you get into bed and one that is still catching up at midnight.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="box-claims" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Why Doesn't the Room Size on the Box Tell You the Time?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Because the box rarely says how many air changes it assumed. The AHAM guideline is that a purifier's smoke CADR should be at least two-thirds of the room's floor area in square feet, assuming an 8 foot ceiling. Do the math and that works out to about 5 air changes per hour, or an air change every 12 minutes. A room clean to about 95 percent takes around 36 minutes on high.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many manufacturers advertise a much bigger coverage area based on fewer air changes, sometimes 2 per hour, sometimes just 1. A unit sold as covering 1,000 square feet at 1 air change per hour is a 200 square foot unit at 5. At 1 air change an hour, getting to about 95 percent takes three hours, which is not what most buyers have in mind. Look for the fine print, or simply compare the CADR to your square footage.
          </p>

          <p>
            CADR is also measured on the highest fan setting. If you plan to run the unit on low or medium for noise, the real delivery rate is lower, often much lower, and the time stretches accordingly. That is why we recommend buying a unit whose CADR comfortably exceeds the AHAM minimum, so it can reach a useful rate at a speed you can sleep through.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ceiling height changes the math too. A 10 foot ceiling adds 25 percent to the volume and a cathedral ceiling can double it. Our guide to <a href="/what-size-air-purifier-do-i-need/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">what size air purifier you need</a> walks through sizing for tall ceilings and open-plan spaces in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="slows" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Makes an Air Purifier Take Longer?
          </h2>

          <p>
            An open door is the biggest one. The purifier is no longer cleaning a 1,440 cubic foot bedroom; it is cleaning the bedroom, the hall and whatever else connects to it, and fresh dirty air flows in as fast as it cleans. Close the door for the fastest result, especially for a bedroom at night or a room during wildfire smoke.
          </p>

          <p>
            A low fan setting is next. On its quietest setting, many purifiers deliver only a fraction of their rated CADR, so a unit that clears a room in 40 minutes on high can take two hours or more on low. Auto mode helps here because it runs fast when the sensor detects particles and drops back once the air is clean.
          </p>

          <p>
            Placement and maintenance matter as well. A unit wedged into a corner or behind a sofa recirculates the same small pocket of air. Give it a foot or two of clear space around the intake and outlet. A clogged pre-filter or an overdue main filter cuts airflow quietly, which lowers the real cleaning rate without any obvious sign. Our guide on <a href="/how-often-to-change-air-purifier-filter/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how often to change an air purifier filter</a> covers the schedule.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, sources. Frying food, a dog shaking off, someone vacuuming, a candle or a window cracked open on a smoky day all add particles faster than a small purifier removes them. The purifier is still working, but the room settles at a higher level. In those moments, turn it up and close the room off rather than assuming it is broken.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pollutants" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Does It Take Longer for Smoke, Dust, Pollen or Odors?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fine smoke particles are where the math above applies most directly. They stay suspended for hours, so the purifier gets many chances to catch them, and a well-sized unit can bring smoke levels down noticeably within half an hour in a closed room. For wildfire season or a smoker in the house, size up and aim for more air changes; our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-smoke/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best air purifiers for smoke</a> are picked on smoke CADR.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dust and pollen behave differently. The larger particles settle within minutes of being stirred up, often before the purifier can reach them. The purifier clears the fine fraction quickly but does little for what has already landed. Run it on high while vacuuming, dusting or changing bedding, when settled particles are briefly airborne.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mold spores sit in between. They are large enough to settle, small enough to stay up for a while, and released intermittently from wherever the mold is growing. A purifier lowers the airborne count within an hour or so but cannot get ahead of an active source. Our guide on <a href="/does-a-hepa-filter-remove-mold-spores/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">whether a HEPA filter removes mold spores</a> covers why.
          </p>

          <p>
            Odors take longest. Smells are gases, which HEPA does not catch at all, so the only thing working on them is the carbon filter, and a thin carbon layer works slowly. Cooking smells in a closed room may fade over an hour or two with a good carbon stage; persistent odors from smoking, pets or mold rarely clear completely while the source remains. Ventilation often works faster for odors than any purifier.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tell" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Can You Tell When the Room Is Actually Clean?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Use a particle monitor if you want a real answer. An inexpensive PM2.5 monitor placed across the room from the purifier shows the particle count falling in real time, and it is the clearest way to see how long your own room takes. Light a match and blow it out, or let the toaster run a little long, then watch how many minutes the reading takes to return to baseline.
          </p>

          <p>
            Built-in sensors are a rougher guide. Most purifiers have a simple particle sensor that drives the color ring and auto mode. They are useful for spotting when something has spiked the air, but they sit right next to the intake, so they can show clean air in the purifier's own neighborhood before the far side of the room has caught up.
          </p>

          <p>
            Your nose is the worst guide. It cannot detect fine particles at all, and it gets used to odors within minutes. A room can smell fine while the particle count is high, and it can keep smelling musty after the particles are gone, because the smell is a gas the HEPA filter does not touch.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">RULE OF THUMB</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Three air changes for clean, five for close to complete.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Divide room volume by smoke CADR for minutes per air change. Multiply by three and you have a realistic time to a noticeably cleaner room with the door closed and the fan on high.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="keep-running" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Should You Turn the Purifier Off Once the Room Is Clean?
          </h2>

          <p>
            No. A room does not stay clean once the purifier stops. People, pets, cooking and air leaking in from outside or from the rest of the house bring particles back, and within an hour or two the levels can be close to where they started. The purifier's job is to hold the room at a lower level, which means running continuously.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is easier and cheaper than most people expect. Running on auto or low, most home purifiers use a few watts to a few dozen watts. Our breakdown of <a href="/how-much-electricity-does-an-air-purifier-use/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how much electricity an air purifier uses</a> puts realistic numbers on it, and for most units it comes to a few dollars a month.
          </p>

          <p>
            The better routine is to run it on high for the first 30 to 60 minutes to bring the room down, then let auto mode or a quiet setting hold it there. For a bedroom, start it on high an hour before bed with the door closed, then drop it to a setting you can sleep through. Our guide on <a href="/should-you-run-an-air-purifier-24-7/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">whether to run an air purifier 24/7</a> covers the trade-offs, including filter life and noise.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your room never seems to get clean, the purifier is probably too small for it. Compare its smoke CADR to the room's square footage, and if it falls well short of two-thirds, look at a bigger unit. Our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-home/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best air purifiers for home</a> are ranked with honest CADR figures so the math above actually works.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the coverage area on the box</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Coverage claims are often calculated at one or two air changes an hour, which can mean hours to clean a room. Divide your room's square footage by the smoke CADR instead. If the CADR is not at least two-thirds of your floor area, expect the room to take much longer than the marketing suggests.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running it with the door open</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                With the door open, a bedroom purifier is trying to clean the whole floor, and dirty air flows in as fast as it cleans. For the quick 30 to 60 minute result, close the door. Open-plan spaces need a larger unit or more than one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging by smell</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Your nose cannot detect fine particles and adapts to odors within minutes. A room can smell fine with a high particle count, or stay musty after the particles are gone. Use a PM2.5 monitor, or trust the arithmetic, rather than deciding by smell whether the purifier is working.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the quietest setting from the start</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                CADR is measured on the highest speed. On low, many units deliver only a fraction of that, so cleaning time can stretch to several hours. Run on high or auto first to bring the room down, then drop to a quiet setting to hold it there.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Switching it off as soon as the air is clean</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Particles return within an hour or two once the purifier stops, from people, pets, cooking and air leaking in. The real benefit comes from holding the room at a low level all day and night. Leave it on auto or low rather than cycling it on and off.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many air changes per hour do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For general air quality and allergies, 4 to 5 air changes per hour is a sensible target and is what the AHAM two-thirds sizing rule delivers. For smoke, wildfire season or asthma, aim higher, around 5 to 6 or more. Multiply the purifier's CADR by 60 and divide by your room's volume in cubic feet to see what yours delivers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do air purifiers work faster in small rooms?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, because the same CADR divided into a smaller volume gives more air changes per hour. A 150 CFM unit cleans a 10 by 10 foot room about three times as fast as a 15 by 20 foot room. That is why a modest unit in a closed bedroom often feels more effective than a large one in an open-plan living area.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it better to run one big purifier or two small ones?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In a single closed room, one unit with the same total CADR does about as well as two. In a larger or oddly shaped space, or two connected rooms, two smaller units placed apart usually clean more evenly because air does not mix perfectly. For separate bedrooms, one appropriately sized unit per room works best.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should an air purifier run before I notice a difference?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                With a correctly sized unit on high and the door closed, most people notice cleaner air within 30 to 60 minutes, especially with smoke or cooking particles. Allergy relief is more gradual and usually shows over several days to a couple of weeks of continuous running, because exposure has to stay low, not just dip once.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does ceiling height affect how long it takes?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, directly. Sizing charts and the AHAM rule assume an 8 foot ceiling. A 10 foot ceiling has 25 percent more air, so every cleaning time is about 25 percent longer at the same CADR. For tall or vaulted ceilings, calculate the actual volume and size the purifier from that, not from floor area alone.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will an air purifier clean the whole house if I leave doors open?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only very slowly, and only if it is large. A portable purifier is rated for one room, and in a whole house its air changes per hour fall to a fraction of what it gives a closed bedroom. For whole-house cleaning, a better HVAC filter with the system fan running does more, with portable units in the most-used rooms.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How quickly does an air purifier remove cooking smells?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Particles from cooking, such as smoke from searing, clear in roughly the same 30 to 60 minutes as other fine particles. The smell itself is gas and depends on the carbon filter, so it clears more slowly, often over an hour or two with a good carbon stage. A range hood vented outside removes cooking smells far faster than any purifier.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

